diff --git a/docs/nntp-fallback-servers.md b/docs/nntp-fallback-servers.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b97edf6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/nntp-fallback-servers.md
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# Fallback newsservers
+
+Spotweb uses one newsserver per task: retrieving spots and comments (`hdr`),
+NZB files and images (`bin`) and posting (`post`). When that server is down,
+or does not have an article because of a short retention or a block account,
+the retrieve run or the download fails.
+
+Fallback newsservers fill that gap. They are set under Settings, Newsservers,
+below the regular newsservers, and stored in the `nntp_fallback` setting. Each
+fallback server has a priority (0 is the highest, 99 the lowest) and a choice
+of tasks it is used for. The regular newsserver of a task has priority 0 and
+goes first when priorities are equal. The model follows SABnzbd's server
+priorities.
+
+## Behaviour per kind of command
+
+- **Reads by message-id** (`HEAD`, `BODY`, `ARTICLE` and the pipelined
+  article fetch): a try-list per article. When a server answers 430 or fails,
+  the next server in priority order is asked for the articles that are still
+  missing. Results keep their original order. Only when no server has an
+  article does the 430 reach the caller.
+- **Reads by article number** (`GROUP`, `XOVER`, `XHDR`): article numbers
+  differ between servers, so these stick to the first server that accepts the
+  `GROUP` and never switch halfway through a session. A later run may pick a
+  different server; `Services_Retriever_Base::searchMessageId` already checks
+  that the stored article number still points to the stored message-id and
+  searches by message-id otherwise, so no spots are skipped or duplicated.
+  The first run after a switch is slower.
+- **Posting**: only moves on when connecting or logging in fails. Once a
+  `POST` has started it is never repeated on another server, so a spot or
+  comment cannot end up on Usenet twice.
+
+A server that cannot be connected to, or that rejects the login, is skipped
+for a while (10 minutes after a timeout or rejected login, 3 minutes after any
+other failure). Every web request and every retrieve run is a separate PHP
+process, so `Services_Nntp_ServerPenaltyBox` keeps that state in a small JSON
+file in the system temp directory, guarded by `flock()`. When every server is
+being skipped, all of them are tried anyway.
+
+Every failover is logged through `SpotDebug` and, on the command line, written
+to STDERR, so it shows up in the output of `retrieve.php`. Passwords are never
+logged.
+
+## Classes
+
+- `Services_Nntp_FallbackServers`: setting name, form slots, validation and
+  the selection of servers per task.
+- `Services_Nntp_FailoverTransport`: extends `Services_Nntp_PipelinedTransport`
+  so it fits everywhere a transport is expected, and routes every public
+  method to the server(s) described above. It holds one plain transport per
+  server and never speaks NNTP itself.
+- `Services_Nntp_ServerPenaltyBox`: the shared skip list.
+
+`Services_Nntp_ClientPool` wraps the transport of each task with
+`Services_Nntp_FailoverTransport::wrap()`. Without fallback servers for a task,
+`wrap()` returns the plain transport, so installations that do not use the
+feature behave exactly as before. No database schema change is needed; the
+settings upgrade only adds an empty `nntp_fallback`.
+
+## Tests
+
+`tests/Services/Nntp/ServicesNntpFailoverTransportTest.php` runs the
+transport against local fixture servers (`tests/Support/NntpFailoverFixtureServer.php`):
+a missing article, an unreachable server, a rejected login, the pipelined
+fetch, sticky group commands, priority order, posting and the client pool.
+`tests/Services/Settings/ServicesSettingsBaseTest.php` covers the form
+validation.
